      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hardware keys primarily defend against remote attacks like phishing, credential stuffing, and man-in-the-middle attacks because they require physical presence. Threat actors cannot simulate the cryptographic challenge and response needed by the key over the internet alone. While sophisticated attacks like physical key skimming or supply chain compromise are theoretical risks, the standard implementation effectively neutralizes the vast majority of common online infiltration methods.",
